Turkey's strong man had looked  invincible but then seemingly from  nowhere and uprising against him in the  form of an attempted coup president erdo  wan's 13-year rule defined by growing  authoritarianism appeared to be over as  the night skies filled with the sound of  jets he is undoubtedly a polarizing  figure revered by his followers but  hated by liberal Turkey the government  claims the rebellious factions in the  military were orchestrated by petula  Gulen the reclusive former ally of  president Erdogan who is now in  self-imposed exile at his headquarters  in the United States denies the  accusations but his hizmet movement does  have millions of devoted followers in  Turkey many have key positions in the  police and the Judiciary this is what  Iran's government means when they talk  of shadowy forces operating within the  system Erdogan came to power in 2003 and  has spent the last few years bolstering  his presidential remit his critics see  him as an enemy of democracy basic  rights have been curtailed with protests  sometimes violence staged against the  suppression of press freedom and  political debate turkey is also  suffering from a rise in terrorist  attacks odo and Syria policy is blamed  by many for destabilizing the nation  it's claimed he has empowered extremist  groups like Isis by using jihadists as a  proxy to battle President Assad and  stymie Iranian influence in the region  the unrest in Turkey then is massively  worrying for the West cooperation with  Ankara is vital on dealing with issues  such as the refugee crisis which has  seen hundreds of thousands of desperate  people wash up on Europe  turkey is also crucially a member of  NATO its military the second largest in  the Alliance support for President  durance government's which was  democratically elected has been coming  in from around the world as of this  moment Turkey's cooperation with us in  our counterterrorism efforts in our NATO  obligations and in our regional efforts  with respect to Syria and Isis have not  been affected negatively all of that has  continued as before the failed coup has  opened up huge fishes in Turkey's  political fabric in the short term  democracy people on the streets  defeating men with guns is the winner  but in the long term freedom will likely  be the loser as president erdo and  strengthens his hold on the country and  cracks down on any and all dissent Alex  Rossi Sky News
 

